["New Jersey's D+4 partisan lean is the only quantitative evidence available", "No state polls, expert ratings, or fundraising figures exist yet", "Open-seat race with a term-limited president removes incumbency effects for both parties", "Headlines reflect national 2028 candidate speculation, not New Jersey-specific signal"], "rationale": "With no polls, ratings, or fundraising to weigh, the forecast rests on New Jersey's D+4 partisan lean, which points to a modest Democratic edge in an open race. Uncertainty stays wide because neither side has a settled nominee and no state-level polling exists.", "watch": "Early general-election polls once nominees emerge, or a sharp shift in the national environment, could move this forecast substantially.", "confidence": "low", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "New Jersey enters the 2028 presidential race as a modest Democratic-leaning state, with a partisan lean of about four points Democratic. No candidate is settled on either side, no general-election polls of the state are available, and no expert ratings have been issued, so the outlook rests almost entirely on the state's underlying partisanship. The open nature of the contest, with the incumbent president term-limited, removes any incumbency advantage for either party.\n\nRecent headlines are dominated by national speculation about potential 2028 contenders, including a possible third bid from New Jersey's own Chris Christie, but none of this amounts to state-level evidence. The forecast therefore expects a Democratic margin of roughly four points, with wide uncertainty given the absence of polling. A favorable national environment for Republicans could easily narrow or flip the state.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.6761, "margin": 4.0}